      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.582,
            0.048,
            0.861,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Some Geometric Themes"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.429,
            0.086,
            0.57,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.435,
                0.198,
                0.565,
                0.214
              ],
              "content": "Sierpinski Carpet"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.231,
            0.847,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "？ Draw the initial few steps (at least till Step 2) of the shape sequence that leads to the Sierpinski Carpet."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.274,
            0.634,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "By its construction, each step in the sequence has"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.297,
            0.848,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "(i) squares of the same size that remain in the figure, and the size of these squares becomes smaller and smaller as the step number increases, and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.358,
            0.782,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "(ii) square holes that are formed by removing square pieces."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.388,
            0.845,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "？ Do you see any pattern in the number of holes and squares that remain at each step?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.431,
            0.845,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "Let $R _ { n }$ represent the number of remaining squares at the nth step, and $H _ { n }$ represent the number of holes at the nth step."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.468,
            0.845,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "Let us understand how these numbers grow by analysing how the holes and squares that remain are generated from the previous step."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.506,
            0.845,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "Every square that remains at a given step, say Step n, gives rise to 8 squares that remain at the (n + 1)th step. Thus, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.546,
            0.555,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "R_{n + 1} = 8 R_n",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.575,
            0.544,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "Can this be used to get a formula for $R _ { n ^ { \\sharp } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.601,
            0.245,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": "We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.619,
            0.576,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { l } R _ { 0 } = 1 \\\\ R _ { 1 } = 8 \\times 1 = 8 \\\\ R _ { 2 } = 8 \\times 8 = 8 ^ { 2 } . \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.674,
            0.332,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": "In general, $R _ { _ n } = 8 _ { _ \\odot } ^ { n }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.703,
            0.758,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, how do we find the number of holes at a given step?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.729,
            0.846,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "Every square that remains at the nth step gives rise to a hole in the (n + 1)th step. All the holes present at the nth step remain in the (n + 1)th step as well. Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.802,
            0.269,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "So we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.399,
            0.784,
            0.712,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { c } { { H _ { _ { n \\; + \\; 1 } } = H _ { _ { n } } + R _ { _ { n } } . } } \\\\ { { } } \\\\ { { R _ { _ { 0 } } = 1 } } \\\\ { { R _ { _ { 1 } } = 8 } } \\\\ { { R _ { _ { 2 } } = 8 ^ { 2 } } } \\\\ { { R _ { _ { 3 } } = 8 ^ { 3 } } } \\end{array} \\begin{array} { c } { { H _ { _ { 0 } } = 0 } } \\\\ { { H _ { _ { 1 } } = 1 } } \\\\ { { H _ { _ { 2 } } = 1 } } \\\\ { { H _ { _ { 3 } } = 1 + 8 } } \\\\ { { H _ { _ { 3 } } = 1 + 8 + 8 ^ { 2 } } } \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.878,
            0.913,
            0.908,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": "71"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
